Order matters in matrix multiplication. 
To multiply two matrices we must have: 

		Number of columns of lef matrix = number of rows of right matrix.

If A is 2 x 3 and B is 3 x 3, then AB is defined (and is a 2 x 3 matrix), but BA is not. 
However, even if both products AB and BA are defined and of the same dimensions (as is the case if A and B both n x n, for example), it is in general still true that 

			AB != BA

Despite the negative news, matrix multiplication does behave well in a numer of respects

One simple operation on matrices that has no analogue in the real number system is the transpose. 
THe transpose of an m x n matrix A is the n x m matrix A^T obtained by writing thr rows of A as columns. 
For example, if 

		A = {1, 2, 3}		then A^T = {1, 4}
		    {4, 5, 6}, 			   {2, 5}
						   {3, 6}

The transpose will largely function as a notational convenience for us. 
